A||||8||billfredmike24||What do you use when repairing panels??||||1||
Z||000000||billfredmike24||08-08-2001||04:36 PM||||What's the best thing to use to repair the fiero.  I have a cracked a-pillar, cracked drivers side ground effect, piece broke off around top of drivers side door panel, and cracks around the trunk lock.  What should i used to fix these(exact name of products would be helpful).  <BR>Thanks MIke<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_10.jpg"> <BR>yeah the wheels are ugly, and the stickers, that'll change||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||08-08-2001||04:49 PM||||without seeing the exact damage, id say fiberglass, or body filler with fiberglass strands in it (NAPA Fibre Hair #6369) after roughing it in, use a lite coat of 3M Flowable finish filler (3M #05824) then sand surrounding area and prime. resand and paint.<P>I kinda like the wheels, theyd look good on a dark blue or color like lisas.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 08-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||billfredmike24||08-08-2001||11:41 PM||||Thanks for the info roger, i take it everyone else fixes'em the same way.<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000003||88CoupeV6||08-09-2001||10:00 AM||||I found this stuff at autozone. It is a 2 part epoxy. It worked great on my bumper. I used it all over the car on any imperfections before it went to the paint shop. It had a 2 inch bumper tear. I also filled the holes for the Word pontiac I removed.<BR>Here is a pic.<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fbr.jpg">   <BR><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">   <BR><B>Also 88 Yellow GT</B> :D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||7
Z||000004||rogergarrison||08-09-2001||01:43 PM||||those are great for Flexible parts like bumper covers. wouldnt recommend you use it on hard surface though.||||reg||1
Z||000005||88CoupeV6||08-09-2001||01:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>those are great for Flexible parts like bumper covers. wouldnt recommend you use it on hard surface though.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Why not? I think it will make them less prone to cracking later. Like bondo or other hard filler would.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||08-09-2001||05:51 PM||||just my opinion, but the flexible filler is somewhat soft. i havent tried it but seems that the sanded/feathered edges would be prone to lifting under the paint. all body filler is somewhat flexible these days, wouldnt be enough flex in roof post or trunk to cause any problem.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||08-09-2001||05:59 PM||||Which would be better for doors and front fenders? Those seem to flex quite a bit, though maybe not as much as a bumper.||||reg||5
Z||000008||rogergarrison||08-09-2001||07:46 PM||||like i said above, on body panels i usually use the filler with glass strands in it, comes in short or long strands. thats what I use on Corvettes too unless a panel is actually broken, then i use glass resin and matting, followed with the reg filler.||||reg||1
